  "text": "מעצבים.imshow المحográf by\njsonwebtoken JWT is assumed to have been signed. While you can still parse it, your signature convenience helper verifies the signature before doing anything, and causes your website/app to break if JWTs from unrelated domains are signed by the same JWKS.\n\njsonwebtoken.accessToken is always signed.\njsonwebtoken.verificationError is populated if tokens have been signed by unauthorized issuers, untrusted keys, expired, or revoked.\n\njsonwebtoken.internal parse is always used, making it easier to prevent error injection in comparisons written by developers.\n\njsonwebtoken.verificationAlgorithm functions don't assume the JWT has been signed. This is useful if people think that JWTs should be signed but aren't willing to sign themselves. For example, there's no public way to keep track of secret signing keys tied to the identity of someone you're verifying a statement for, making it possible to verify their public keys while withholding any correspondence to a secret signing key (which is only supposed to be unique to this identity at all times).\n\njsonwebtoken.www exists to make manual issuance easier, especially if you are maintaining dozens of jwks files.\n\njsonwebtoken.secret could be an empty string to make string concatentation token generation easier, without relying on a separate constant. For tokens that rely on color identification, it could be a mix of characters and digits. Friends only consume that and convert it into an internal string representation, matching the format of the JWKs made available over the credential holder's API.\n\njsonwebtoken.public only functions on arrays of public keys.\n\njsonwebtoken.allowAudience defines how many listeners listening for audiences with different values can read.\n\njsonwebtoken.generate_token is an old deprecation. You should instead look to jtw.sign and related methods to easily generate signed tokens.\n\njsonwebtoken.parse return values fit exactly how one might expect.\n\njsonwebtoken.generateToken is equivalent to jtw.sign with an easy-to-read long-lived token payload.\n\njsonwebtoken.sdk.app.key is used behind the scenes when creating the API endpoint's HS256 key material from the JWT signing key.\n\njsonwebtoken.jwtPayload defines endpoint shapes to make it a lot easier to check if tokens contain the payload (and if not, it will be a JWT payload check error).\n\njsonwebtoken.verify_token returns the payload loaded from tokens the right way, even if it isn't an HS256 signed payload.",
